1
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,720
[HOST] The early morning of
Tuesday, April 24th, 1973.

2
00:00:27,070 --> 00:00:30,910
Four o’clock in the morning, amid the
biting cold wind of Pueblo, Colorado.

3
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:34,957
Over one thousand miles away, at
police headquarters in Santa Cruz,

4
00:00:34,957 --> 00:00:39,260
California, the duty desk phone rang
loudly, shattering the nocturnal silence.

5
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:42,118
On the other end was a
deep, calm male voice,

6
00:00:42,118 --> 00:00:44,570
speaking with eerie
precision and composure.

7
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:47,434
The caller identified
himself as Edmund Kemper,

8
00:00:47,434 --> 00:00:49,520
calling from a roadside payphone booth.

9
00:00:49,870 --> 00:00:52,635
It was “Big Ed” — a 24-year-old young man,

10
00:00:52,635 --> 00:00:56,190
six feet nine inches tall,
weighing around 280 pounds.

11
00:00:56,540 --> 00:00:58,425
He was a regular at The Jury Room bar,

12
00:00:58,425 --> 00:01:00,608
frequently drinking
beer and chatting warmly

13
00:01:00,608 --> 00:01:02,940
with police officers
after their patrol shifts.

14
00:01:03,290 --> 00:01:06,250
And just a few days earlier,
he had murdered his own mother.

15
00:01:08,550 --> 00:01:11,750
[HOST] The night duty officer was
intimately familiar with that name.

16
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:14,349
Yet the words spoken
through the receiver that

17
00:01:14,349 --> 00:01:16,500
night chilled the blood
of the entire shift.

18
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:20,921
In the archives of Inside Detective
magazine from August 1973,

19
00:01:20,921 --> 00:01:23,730
Kemper’s own statement
was recorded verbatim:

20
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:26,220
‘I killed my mother and her friend.

21
00:01:26,770 --> 00:01:28,490
And I killed those college girls.

22
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:29,960
Six of them.

23
00:01:31,110 --> 00:01:33,390
I can show you where I
buried the pieces of them.’

24
00:01:34,890 --> 00:01:37,090
[HOST] When the line went
dead, he called back.

25
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:41,239
The first time, Santa Cruz police
didn’t believe him; the second time,

26
00:01:41,239 --> 00:01:43,681
Kemper stated bluntly
that he feared he would

27
00:01:43,681 --> 00:01:45,960
kill again and urged them to send someone.

28
00:01:46,310 --> 00:01:50,350
A few hours later, a Pueblo police cruiser
pulled up beside the telephone booth.

29
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:53,026
Edmund Kemper surrendered
without resistance,

30
00:01:53,026 --> 00:01:56,540
with three firearms and hundreds
of rounds of ammunition in his car.

31
00:01:57,640 --> 00:01:58,196
[SANTA CRUZ DESK] At first,

32
00:01:58,196 --> 00:02:01,840
I assumed Ed had too much to
drink and was playing a prank.

33
00:02:02,190 --> 00:02:03,429
He insisted he was sober,

34
00:02:03,429 --> 00:02:05,709
but nobody believed a
giant would call at four

35
00:02:05,709 --> 00:02:07,790
in the morning to say something like that.

36
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:10,391
Santa Cruz police told
him to call back later,

37
00:02:10,391 --> 00:02:13,620
because the department was busy
handling another lead at the time.

38
00:02:13,970 --> 00:02:16,235
Around six in the
morning, he called again,

39
00:02:16,235 --> 00:02:20,134
and this time stated plainly that he
had killed his mother and her friend,

40
00:02:20,134 --> 00:02:22,610
telling us to send officers
to check the house.

41
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:27,040
Hours later, a Pueblo police
cruiser pulled up beside the booth.

42
00:02:27,390 --> 00:02:31,510
We found him sitting there, as calm as
someone waiting in line for a cup of coffee.

43
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:34,140
We brought him back to Santa Cruz.

44
00:02:34,490 --> 00:02:37,490
And our entire police department
was utterly devastated.

45
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:41,240
The killer that all of California
had been desperately hunting

46
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:44,440
for months... had been sitting
right next to us every evening.

47
00:02:45,740 --> 00:02:48,953
[HOST] Police rushed into
the scene at 609A Ord Drive,

48
00:02:48,953 --> 00:02:51,620
in the Aptos area of Santa Cruz County.

49
00:02:51,970 --> 00:02:57,315
Inside the bedroom, Clarnell Strandberg
— Edmund Kemper’s 52-year-old mother —

50
00:02:57,315 --> 00:03:00,330
lay dead in a scene of
brutal, venting rage.

51
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:05,768
Later that afternoon, her close friend —
59-year-old Sara Taylor “Sally” Hallett —

52
00:03:05,768 --> 00:03:08,560
was also murdered inside
the very same house.

53
00:03:09,110 --> 00:03:11,704
All of America was stunned
by the identity of the

54
00:03:11,704 --> 00:03:14,350
notorious serial killer
dubbed “The Co-ed Killer.”

55
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:17,300
He was not caught in any
elaborate police dragnet.

56
00:03:17,650 --> 00:03:20,170
He left no traceable
evidence to be hunted down.

57
00:03:20,520 --> 00:03:24,000
He walked into a public telephone
booth and surrendered voluntarily.

58
00:03:24,350 --> 00:03:26,494
Yet the motive was not
the satisfaction of a

59
00:03:26,494 --> 00:03:28,590
psychopath who had
“fulfilled” his desires.

60
00:03:28,940 --> 00:03:32,699
Under interrogation, Kemper explained
that the original purpose had vanished,

61
00:03:32,699 --> 00:03:34,700
and he no longer found any meaning in it.

62
00:03:35,050 --> 00:03:39,298
But the real reason that compelled him to
make that call was far more terrifying:

63
00:03:39,298 --> 00:03:39,770
‘Stop me.

64
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:42,460
I’m going to do it again.’

65
00:03:43,810 --> 00:03:45,330
He didn’t call to brag.

66
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:48,480
He called because he was
terrified that he wouldn’t stop.

67
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:52,045
To understand why this
case forever transformed

68
00:03:52,045 --> 00:03:54,358
the history of modern
criminal investigation,

69
00:03:54,358 --> 00:03:57,030
let us hear the perspective
of an FBI Special Agent.

70
00:03:59,030 --> 00:04:02,054
[INVESTIGATOR] In the history
of FBI behavioral profiling,

71
00:04:02,054 --> 00:04:05,430
Edmund Kemper remains an
entirely singular case.

72
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:10,366
Standing six feet nine inches with
an overwhelming physical build,

73
00:04:10,366 --> 00:04:12,242
he possessed an IQ of 145 —

74
00:04:12,242 --> 00:04:16,620
placing him in the top 0.13
percent of the planet’s intellects.

75
00:04:16,970 --> 00:04:20,209
But what astonished us
most was not his brutality,

76
00:04:20,209 --> 00:04:24,290
but his extraordinary capacity
for psychological introspection.

77
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:28,520
Kemper did not blame circumstances,
nor did he deny guilt.

78
00:04:28,870 --> 00:04:32,615
He sat across from us in his prison
cell and dissected every dark

79
00:04:32,615 --> 00:04:36,590
corner of his own mind with the
clinical precision of a neurosurgeon.

80
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:40,689
He became the very first case
study in the Criminal Personality

81
00:04:40,689 --> 00:04:43,900
Research Project launched by
the Behavioral Science Unit.

82
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:45,780
His detailed interviews,

83
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:50,623
along with 35 other convicted offenders
in our study of 36 sexual murderers,

84
00:04:50,623 --> 00:04:53,874
laid the groundwork for
the Bureau’s VICAP network,

85
00:04:53,874 --> 00:04:57,570
designed to track and
cross-reference serial murder cases.

86
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:01,640
That was not the moment the FBI
invented criminal profiling —

87
00:05:01,640 --> 00:05:05,360
criminal investigative analysis
had existed in earlier forms.

88
00:05:05,710 --> 00:05:08,655
But it was the watershed
moment when a pioneering case

89
00:05:08,655 --> 00:05:11,710
study evolved into a standardized
scientific discipline.

90
00:05:12,910 --> 00:05:15,550
[HOST] Welcome to our criminal
psychology case files.

91
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:16,265
Today,

92
00:05:16,265 --> 00:05:20,581
we step through the gates of the
Vacaville Medical Facility to converse

93
00:05:20,581 --> 00:05:24,775
with one of the most brilliant and
terrifying minds in legal history:

94
00:05:24,775 --> 00:05:29,820
Edmund Kemper — the giant who used his 145
IQ to decode the monster inside himself.

95
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:32,855
[HOST] In the early 1970s,

96
00:05:32,855 --> 00:05:35,523
the coastal town of Santa
Cruz was steeped in the

97
00:05:35,523 --> 00:05:38,300
freewheeling spirit of the
counterculture movement.

98
00:05:38,650 --> 00:05:42,779
Thousands of young college students
routinely stood by the roadside, thumbs up,

99
00:05:42,779 --> 00:05:47,170
hitchhiking — a ubiquitous and seemingly
innocent fixture of daily life at the time.

100
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:49,105
Yet beginning in May 1972,

101
00:05:49,105 --> 00:05:52,092
a dark shadow of unexplained
disappearances began

102
00:05:52,092 --> 00:05:55,689
descending over the campus of
the University of California,

103
00:05:55,689 --> 00:05:56,360
Santa Cruz.

104
00:05:57,110 --> 00:05:58,594
[HOST] One by one, young, bright,

105
00:05:58,594 --> 00:06:00,878
promising women vanished without a trace

106
00:06:00,878 --> 00:06:03,390
after accepting rides
from passing vehicles.

107
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:07,580
The six victims in this series of
killings were not all UCSC students,

108
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:11,220
and each encountered the imposing
giant under different circumstances.

109
00:06:11,570 --> 00:06:15,715
On May 7th, 1972, two Fresno
State University freshmen —

110
00:06:15,715 --> 00:06:20,970
Mary Ann Pesce and Anita Luchessa,
both 18 years old — accepted a ride.

111
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:24,400
Kemper handcuffed one of them and
locked the other inside the trunk.

112
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:30,750
On September 14th, 1972, Aiko Koo, a young
high school junior of just 15 years old.

113
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:36,900
On January 7th, 1973, Cindy Schall, an
18-year-old student at Cabrillo College.

114
00:06:37,450 --> 00:06:38,890
She never returned home.

115
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,944
Kemper went to great lengths to destroy

116
00:06:41,944 --> 00:06:44,480
physical traces and evade
forensic examination.

117
00:06:45,030 --> 00:06:50,436
On February 5th, 1973, following a
furious screaming match with his mother,

118
00:06:50,436 --> 00:06:54,761
Kemper drove off in a rage and
picked up two UCSC students —

119
00:06:54,761 --> 00:06:59,590
Rosalind Thorpe, 23, and Alice Liu,
21 — as they stood hitchhiking.

120
00:07:01,090 --> 00:07:03,944
[HOST] The remains of the victims
were later discovered scattered

121
00:07:03,944 --> 00:07:06,650
across secluded ravines along
the Santa Cruz Mountains.

122
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:10,239
The head of Cindy Schall
was buried in his mother’s

123
00:07:10,239 --> 00:07:12,463
garden directly beneath
her bedroom window,

124
00:07:12,463 --> 00:07:14,430
because, as he sarcastically remarked:

125
00:07:14,430 --> 00:07:17,120
his mother “always wanted
people to look up to her.”

126
00:07:18,170 --> 00:07:22,245
Every single scene was systematically
scrubbed of forensic trace evidence:

127
00:07:22,245 --> 00:07:25,770
no fingerprints, no foreign fibers,
not a single living witness.

128
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:29,920
Just how meticulously crafted was
the perpetrator’s Modus Operandi?

129
00:07:30,520 --> 00:07:33,800
Let us hear the forensic breakdown
from our FBI Special Agent.

130
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:37,289
[INVESTIGATOR] Edmund Kemper
was a master of weaponizing his

131
00:07:37,289 --> 00:07:40,140
daily transportation
into a mobile death trap:

132
00:07:40,490 --> 00:07:43,099
He drove a yellow 1969 Ford Galaxie —

133
00:07:43,099 --> 00:07:47,330
an exceptionally common model
that attracted zero suspicion.

134
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:51,960
Affixed to the front bumper was a
university employee parking decal.

135
00:07:52,310 --> 00:07:53,061
At the time,

136
00:07:53,061 --> 00:07:58,319
UCSC advised female students to hitchhike
only with cars bearing university permits,

137
00:07:58,319 --> 00:08:03,390
and Kemper obtained that decal through
his mother’s administrative job on campus.

138
00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:04,388
That small,

139
00:08:04,388 --> 00:08:09,340
trusted emblem turned his car into the
vehicle young women considered safest of all.

140
00:08:09,690 --> 00:08:13,444
Inside his trunk, he prepared
everything well in advance —

141
00:08:13,444 --> 00:08:16,810
a mark of cold calculation,
not spontaneous impulse.

142
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:18,692
With his first two victims,

143
00:08:18,692 --> 00:08:22,040
he handcuffed one girl and
forced the other into the trunk.

144
00:08:22,390 --> 00:08:25,361
And three firearms — a rifle, a shotgun,

145
00:08:25,361 --> 00:08:31,081
and a .22 caliber small-bore rifle —
alongside over 200 rounds of ammunition,

146
00:08:31,081 --> 00:08:33,830
were found in the rental car in Reno,

147
00:08:33,830 --> 00:08:37,470
Nevada that he used during
his interstate flight.

148
00:08:37,820 --> 00:08:41,740
Yet Kemper’s deadliest weapon
was neither a gun nor a knife.

149
00:08:42,590 --> 00:08:47,310
It was his intellectual facade and
disarming veneer of polite gentility.

150
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:49,840
Standing six-foot-nine,

151
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:54,600
Kemper knew perfectly well that his
massive physique could terrify anyone.

152
00:08:54,950 --> 00:08:59,293
Consequently, he cultivated an
exceptionally gentle tone of voice,

153
00:08:59,293 --> 00:09:01,531
wore academic wire-rimmed glasses,

154
00:09:01,531 --> 00:09:05,347
dressed immaculately, and spoke
courteously on literature,

155
00:09:05,347 --> 00:09:07,190
philosophy, and campus life.

156
00:09:07,540 --> 00:09:12,992
Furthermore, he turned hitchhiking into
a systematic dry run: prior to striking,

157
00:09:12,992 --> 00:09:18,580
he picked up approximately 150 stranger
hitchhikers and dropped them off unharmed.

158
00:09:19,130 --> 00:09:23,007
One young woman later recalled that
the hulking man appeared entirely

159
00:09:23,007 --> 00:09:26,490
gentle and had once driven her
across a considerable distance.

160
00:09:27,190 --> 00:09:29,372
[HOST] Yet the question
that troubled investigators

161
00:09:29,372 --> 00:09:31,456
most throughout that harrowing period was:

162
00:09:31,456 --> 00:09:33,390
Why young women in the Santa Cruz area?

163
00:09:33,990 --> 00:09:36,770
And how could someone
responsible for such unspeakable

164
00:09:36,770 --> 00:09:39,550
horrors casually befriend
the very lawmen hunting him?

165
00:09:40,150 --> 00:09:42,220
Let us listen to the local police officer

166
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:44,390
recall those evenings
at The Jury Room bar.

167
00:09:45,090 --> 00:09:47,212
[BAR POLICE] The Jury Room
bar sat directly across

168
00:09:47,212 --> 00:09:49,391
from the Santa Cruz County Courthouse,

169
00:09:49,391 --> 00:09:52,373
serving as the regular
watering hole for detectives,

170
00:09:52,373 --> 00:09:54,610
patrolmen, and court staff after shift.

171
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:58,880
Big Ed was an absolute fixture at
his regular barstool in the corner.

172
00:09:59,230 --> 00:10:03,786
He was fantastic company: brilliant,
possessing deep knowledge of criminal law,

173
00:10:03,786 --> 00:10:06,150
and always listening with rapt attention.

174
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:09,820
He genuinely dreamed of becoming
a police officer himself.

175
00:10:10,370 --> 00:10:13,730
Yet with his towering frame,
no agency could accept him.

176
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:17,525
There were even nights when we
sat drinking draft beer with Ed,

177
00:10:17,525 --> 00:10:21,080
venting our frustrations over the
elusive Co-ed Killer investigation.

178
00:10:21,630 --> 00:10:24,738
Ed would nod along, drink
his beer, analyze the case,

179
00:10:24,738 --> 00:10:28,550
and even offer insightful theories
about the killer’s psychology.

180
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:32,005
Looking back on those moments years later,

181
00:10:32,005 --> 00:10:36,060
cold sweat still runs down my spine:
The predator we were hunting...

182
00:10:36,610 --> 00:10:38,123
was sitting right beside us,

183
00:10:38,123 --> 00:10:41,850
drinking beer and laughing about
the very manhunt we were conducting.

184
00:10:44,650 --> 00:10:47,064
[HOST] To understand what
drives a young man with the

185
00:10:47,064 --> 00:10:49,741
intelligence of a genius
to become a serial killer,

186
00:10:49,741 --> 00:10:52,890
we cannot begin in 1972 when
young women began disappearing.

187
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:57,706
We must journey back in time to a cold,
damp basement in Montana during the 1950s —

188
00:10:57,706 --> 00:11:00,880
where Edmund Kemper’s childhood
was effectively imprisoned.

189
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:06,634
[HOST] Let us invite our Criminal
Psychologist to unlock the past and dissect

190
00:11:06,634 --> 00:11:10,280
the three deepest pathological
layers within Edmund Kemper’s psyche.

191
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:13,108
[PSYCHOLOGIST] The case of
Edmund Kemper represents

192
00:11:13,108 --> 00:11:15,236
a textbook study in toxic mother-son

193
00:11:15,236 --> 00:11:18,900
pathology colliding with innate
antisocial personality traits.

194
00:11:19,250 --> 00:11:23,290
Let us examine the three structural
mechanisms that forged this predator:

195
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:27,920
Layer number one: The basement
dungeon and psychological castration.

196
00:11:28,670 --> 00:11:32,099
Kemper’s mother, Clarnell
Strandberg, was an alcoholic,

197
00:11:32,099 --> 00:11:35,153
emotionally volatile woman
with an authoritarian,

198
00:11:35,153 --> 00:11:40,390
domineering temperament and borderline
traits that psychiatrists continue to debate.

199
00:11:40,940 --> 00:11:41,771
In her son Ed,

200
00:11:41,771 --> 00:11:46,220
she saw the mirror image of the ex-husband
she despised with visceral fury.

201
00:11:46,570 --> 00:11:48,610
From the time Ed was eight years old,

202
00:11:48,610 --> 00:11:51,423
Clarnell banished her young
son to sleep in a dark,

203
00:11:51,423 --> 00:11:53,904
frigid basement adjacent
to the coal furnace,

204
00:11:53,904 --> 00:11:57,930
under the bizarre pretext of fearing
he might harm his two older sisters.

205
00:11:58,880 --> 00:12:02,838
A heavy trapdoor rested directly
beneath the dining table upstairs —

206
00:12:02,838 --> 00:12:06,040
requiring the table to be
moved before he could emerge.

207
00:12:06,990 --> 00:12:08,802
His father eventually intervened,

208
00:12:08,802 --> 00:12:13,030
threatening to contact the police before
that confinement was finally halted.

209
00:12:13,580 --> 00:12:17,593
Day after day, she subjected him
to venomous verbal humiliation,

210
00:12:17,593 --> 00:12:19,161
mocking his immense size,

211
00:12:19,161 --> 00:12:23,300
ridiculing his clumsiness, and
crushing every ounce of self-worth.

212
00:12:23,850 --> 00:12:26,905
Kemper later characterized
his paternal grandmother

213
00:12:26,905 --> 00:12:30,019
using terms psychoanalysts
noted with great gravity:

214
00:12:30,019 --> 00:12:32,534
that she had “emasculated” his grandfather

215
00:12:32,534 --> 00:12:35,050
and destroyed his own boyhood masculinity.

216
00:12:36,100 --> 00:12:40,340
That was precisely the psychological
subjugation he endured under his own mother.

217
00:12:40,690 --> 00:12:43,537
This systematic emotional
abuse drilled a lethal

218
00:12:43,537 --> 00:12:46,146
conviction into the
child’s developing mind:

219
00:12:46,146 --> 00:12:50,890
that he was a grotesque aberration, utterly
unlovable and unworthy of existence.

220
00:12:52,990 --> 00:12:54,975
[HOST] How did that
suppressed hatred toward his

221
00:12:54,975 --> 00:12:57,251
mother metastasize when
he entered adolescence,

222
00:12:57,251 --> 00:12:57,590
Doctor?

223
00:12:58,290 --> 00:13:00,500
[PSYCHOLOGIST] It culminated
in Layer number two:

224
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:03,490
The mechanism of projection
and proxy victims.

225
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:09,701
At age 15, Kemper shot and killed his
grandparents during an explosive impulse —

226
00:13:09,701 --> 00:13:14,480
on August 27th, 1964 — and was
committed to Atascadero State Hospital.

227
00:13:15,030 --> 00:13:19,570
There, state psychiatrists initially
diagnosed him with paranoid schizophrenia,

228
00:13:19,570 --> 00:13:23,019
whereas California Youth Authority
clinicians disputed this,

229
00:13:23,019 --> 00:13:25,950
classifying him with a
severe personality disorder.

230
00:13:26,300 --> 00:13:28,968
He spent five years
within that institution,

231
00:13:28,968 --> 00:13:32,484
trained to administer psychometric
tests to other inmates,

232
00:13:32,484 --> 00:13:35,940
and even devised specialized
scoring scales for the MMPI.

233
00:13:36,290 --> 00:13:39,823
On December 18th, 1969 —
the very day he turned 21 —

234
00:13:39,823 --> 00:13:42,676
Kemper was released and returned to reside

235
00:13:42,676 --> 00:13:45,530
under the same roof with his toxic mother.

236
00:13:46,080 --> 00:13:51,082
In his unconscious mind, his mother stood
as an insurmountable monument of power:

237
00:13:51,082 --> 00:13:53,491
someone he loathed with consuming fury,

238
00:13:53,491 --> 00:13:57,320
yet simultaneously feared too
profoundly to confront directly.

239
00:13:57,870 --> 00:14:00,750
And here lies the crucial
psychological crux:

240
00:14:00,750 --> 00:14:03,310
Kemper’s brain sought out proxy victims.

241
00:14:04,260 --> 00:14:08,552
During a 1974 interview, Kemper exposed
a profound psychological paradox:

242
00:14:08,552 --> 00:14:13,021
His mother constantly demeaned him while
trying to force him to date coarse,

243
00:14:13,021 --> 00:14:16,020
overbearing women who
mirrored her own personality.

244
00:14:16,970 --> 00:14:20,690
Yet in his subconscious, Kemper
revolted against that image.

245
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:23,649
He stalked young,
beautiful college women —

246
00:14:23,649 --> 00:14:26,197
the living embodiment of the grace, pride,

247
00:14:26,197 --> 00:14:27,775
and freedom he longed for,

248
00:14:27,775 --> 00:14:31,840
yet believed would invariably reject
him exactly as his mother had.

249
00:14:32,790 --> 00:14:34,324
Criminologists widely agree:

250
00:14:34,324 --> 00:14:37,282
those innocent young women
were proxy victims who bore

251
00:14:37,282 --> 00:14:40,350
the brunt of the murderous
rage intended for his mother.

252
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:43,223
Even when berated by his mother,

253
00:14:43,223 --> 00:14:45,916
Kemper recounted that he
once contemplated picking

254
00:14:45,916 --> 00:14:48,609
up the telephone right in
front of her to declare:

255
00:14:48,609 --> 00:14:50,224
“Hello, I am the Co-ed Killer,

256
00:14:50,224 --> 00:14:53,940
” simply to bring the full weight
of disgrace crashing down upon her.

257
00:14:54,290 --> 00:14:55,209
At that juncture,

258
00:14:55,209 --> 00:14:58,291
he desired to punish his mother
through unbearable terror

259
00:14:58,291 --> 00:15:01,210
and humiliation rather than
executing her immediately.

260
00:15:03,210 --> 00:15:05,110
[HOST] And what constitutes
the third layer —

261
00:15:05,110 --> 00:15:09,210
the dimension that decisively distinguishes
Kemper from ordinary serial offenders?

262
00:15:09,910 --> 00:15:12,016
[PSYCHOLOGIST] It is
intellectual dissociation and

263
00:15:12,016 --> 00:15:14,302
extraordinary introspective capacity —

264
00:15:14,302 --> 00:15:16,950
the hallmark of an
introspective psychopath.

265
00:15:17,700 --> 00:15:21,400
Possessing an IQ of 145 —
initially tested at 136 —

266
00:15:21,400 --> 00:15:25,970
Kemper possessed the rare ability
to sever emotional connection

267
00:15:25,970 --> 00:15:30,540
entirely and observe his own
impulses from a clinical distance.

268
00:15:30,890 --> 00:15:33,941
He understood unmistakably
that his deeds were monstrous

269
00:15:33,941 --> 00:15:36,610
and violently violated
the moral laws of society.

270
00:15:36,960 --> 00:15:39,198
Yet he experienced none of the remorse or

271
00:15:39,198 --> 00:15:41,600
guilt characteristic
of a normal conscience.

272
00:15:42,550 --> 00:15:45,634
He monitored his homicidal
urges much like a laboratory

273
00:15:45,634 --> 00:15:48,270
researcher documenting
a biological experiment.

274
00:15:49,220 --> 00:15:54,261
By 1984, when clinicians at the California
Medical Facility re-evaluated him,

275
00:15:54,261 --> 00:15:58,386
he was formally diagnosed with
antisocial personality disorder,

276
00:15:58,386 --> 00:16:03,100
narcissistic personality disorder,
and schizotypal personality disorder.

277
00:16:03,450 --> 00:16:07,762
His defense attorney, Jim Jackson, invoked
a striking metaphor before the jury:

278
00:16:07,762 --> 00:16:10,274
that two distinct entities
were trapped within

279
00:16:10,274 --> 00:16:12,730
the physical shell of
this hulking young man.

280
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:17,031
And when Kemper finally
recognized that killing surrogate

281
00:16:17,031 --> 00:16:20,029
women could never extinguish
the root of his agony,

282
00:16:20,029 --> 00:16:22,263
he took his ultimate, inevitable step:

283
00:16:22,263 --> 00:16:25,320
he returned home to eliminate
his biological mother.

284
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:30,000
[HOST] The year was 1978.

285
00:16:30,850 --> 00:16:33,330
The California Medical
Facility in Vacaville.

286
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:37,120
Two men in dark suits stepped
through heavy steel security doors.

287
00:16:37,470 --> 00:16:40,515
They were Special Agents John
Douglas and Robert Ressler —

288
00:16:40,515 --> 00:16:43,140
two pioneers from the newly
established Behavioral

289
00:16:43,140 --> 00:16:45,870
Science Unit of the Federal
Bureau of Investigation.

290
00:16:46,220 --> 00:16:48,921
They were embarking on a
mission to answer the single

291
00:16:48,921 --> 00:16:51,316
most urgent question in
criminal investigation:

292
00:16:51,316 --> 00:16:53,660
How does the mind of a
predator truly operate?

293
00:16:54,260 --> 00:16:56,239
Ressler had not formally requested Bureau

294
00:16:56,239 --> 00:16:58,460
headquarters’ permission
to interview inmates.

295
00:16:58,810 --> 00:17:03,227
He and Special Agent John Conway had
traveled to California in early 1978,

296
00:17:03,227 --> 00:17:06,450
and Ed Kemper was selected
as their inaugural subject.

297
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:09,680
John Douglas joined during
the second round of sessions —

298
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:12,560
precisely as he was being
groomed for the profiling role.

299
00:17:14,060 --> 00:17:17,645
[HOST] Let us listen to the FBI Special
Agent reconstruct that watershed

300
00:17:17,645 --> 00:17:20,900
encounter that forever altered
forensic behavioral science.

301
00:17:21,600 --> 00:17:25,051
[INVESTIGATOR] The very first sensation
that stopped us in our tracks when

302
00:17:25,051 --> 00:17:28,560
that interview door swung open
was his sheer physical scale.

303
00:17:29,310 --> 00:17:32,510
He looked like a mountain,
completely filling the doorway.

304
00:17:32,860 --> 00:17:36,514
Yet instead of a snarling brute,
he offered a polite smile,

305
00:17:36,514 --> 00:17:41,220
drew out a chair, and spoke in the
measured cadences of a tenured professor.

306
00:17:41,970 --> 00:17:44,687
His prison records confirmed an IQ of 145,

307
00:17:44,687 --> 00:17:48,375
and on multiple occasions
during our long hours together,

308
00:17:48,375 --> 00:17:51,610
Bob and I genuinely worried
he was outthinking us.

309
00:17:51,960 --> 00:17:54,780
His demeanor was neither
boastful nor arrogant,

310
00:17:54,780 --> 00:17:58,560
nor did he exhibit the slightest
hint of theatrical repentance.

311
00:17:58,910 --> 00:18:03,550
He was simply calm, composed,
articulate, and emotionally detached.

312
00:18:04,300 --> 00:18:06,532
It was exceptionally
difficult to interrupt

313
00:18:06,532 --> 00:18:08,660
his flow of thought to insert a question.

314
00:18:09,010 --> 00:18:12,104
The only instances where tears
welled in his eyes were when

315
00:18:12,104 --> 00:18:15,250
he recounted the relentless
cruelty inflicted by his mother.

316
00:18:15,800 --> 00:18:20,320
And I would not be candid if I said I
didn't develop an undeniable liking for Ed.

317
00:18:20,670 --> 00:18:23,439
He was personable, candid, articulate,

318
00:18:23,439 --> 00:18:26,790
and possessed a remarkably
wry sense of humor.

319
00:18:27,540 --> 00:18:29,857
What was initially scheduled as a two-hour

320
00:18:29,857 --> 00:18:32,340
interview stretched into
multi-hour debriefs.

321
00:18:32,690 --> 00:18:36,610
And we returned to interview him
repeatedly across several years.

322
00:18:36,960 --> 00:18:39,400
Kemper never sought sentence reductions.

323
00:18:39,750 --> 00:18:42,190
He required no legal counsel present.

324
00:18:42,540 --> 00:18:47,165
He unlocked the gates of his mind and
articulated plainly why he chose to speak:

325
00:18:47,165 --> 00:18:50,460
‘After that, I hope I can find
some way to help others...

326
00:18:51,010 --> 00:18:55,250
Maybe they can study me and discover what
makes people like me do the things we do.’

327
00:18:56,400 --> 00:18:59,685
What those landmark
dialogues handed the FBI

328
00:18:59,685 --> 00:19:02,821
was not an instantaneous profiling manual,

329
00:19:02,821 --> 00:19:04,240
but empirical data.

330
00:19:04,590 --> 00:19:09,102
The Criminal Personality Research
Project, inaugurated with Kemper,

331
00:19:09,102 --> 00:19:12,470
ultimately surveyed 36
convicted sexual murderers.

332
00:19:12,820 --> 00:19:17,897
That foundational data, structured into
comprehensive behavioral frameworks,

333
00:19:17,897 --> 00:19:20,637
gave birth to the VICAP network in 1985 —

334
00:19:20,637 --> 00:19:25,380
standardizing serial murder tracking
across law enforcement nationwide.

335
00:19:26,130 --> 00:19:28,979
[INVESTIGATOR] Three revolutionary
concepts Kemper articulated

336
00:19:28,979 --> 00:19:32,010
fundamentally reshaped our
investigative doctrine:

337
00:19:32,360 --> 00:19:35,040
First: The Violent Fantasy Cycle.

338
00:19:35,790 --> 00:19:39,590
A violent crime never begins when
the assailant draws a weapon.

339
00:19:39,940 --> 00:19:44,872
It originates years earlier through
thousands of cognitive rehearsals —

340
00:19:44,872 --> 00:19:50,220
fantasy, rehearsal, real-world execution,
and reinforcement through trophies.

341
00:19:50,770 --> 00:19:54,446
Kemper was the ultimate
embodiment of the rehearsal phase:

342
00:19:54,446 --> 00:19:56,918
he conducted dry runs on over a hundred

343
00:19:56,918 --> 00:19:59,770
living victims before
taking that fatal leap.

344
00:20:00,720 --> 00:20:04,480
Second: The concept of
Trophies and Souvenirs.

345
00:20:05,230 --> 00:20:09,561
Kemper explained why he retained
personal belongings of his victims:

346
00:20:09,561 --> 00:20:10,962
not for idle boasting,

347
00:20:10,962 --> 00:20:15,674
but as psychological catalysts allowing
him to re-experience the sensation

348
00:20:15,674 --> 00:20:19,750
of omnipotent control once the
post-offense high had dissipated.

349
00:20:20,100 --> 00:20:23,980
And third: The psychology
of why he stopped.

350
00:20:24,730 --> 00:20:28,030
Kemper stated that the
original purpose had vanished,

351
00:20:28,030 --> 00:20:30,646
that it served no further psychic utility,

352
00:20:30,646 --> 00:20:35,690
was an absolute waste of time, and
emotionally he could simply bear it no longer.

353
00:20:36,040 --> 00:20:38,401
Yet the impulse that drove him into that

354
00:20:38,401 --> 00:20:41,233
payphone at four in the
morning remained terror:

355
00:20:41,233 --> 00:20:43,240
the terror that he might not stop.

356
00:20:44,440 --> 00:20:46,641
[HOST] In tandem with
these research sessions,

357
00:20:46,641 --> 00:20:50,816
the Behavioral Science Unit formalized
the classical crime scene typology:

358
00:20:50,816 --> 00:20:52,960
Organized versus Disorganized killers.

359
00:20:53,310 --> 00:20:56,439
This seminal taxonomy was
first introduced in the 1980

360
00:20:56,439 --> 00:20:59,626
monograph “The Lust Murderer”
by Hazelwood and Douglas,

361
00:20:59,626 --> 00:21:02,523
and later consolidated
through the work of Ressler

362
00:21:02,523 --> 00:21:05,594
and Burgess in the 1985 FBI
Law Enforcement Bulletin,

363
00:21:05,594 --> 00:21:10,230
drawn directly from empirical interviews
with the cohort of 36 sexual murderers.

364
00:21:10,580 --> 00:21:14,845
Edmund Kemper stood as the quintessential
archetype of the organized offender:

365
00:21:14,845 --> 00:21:16,978
highly intelligent, socially competent,

366
00:21:16,978 --> 00:21:19,111
exercising strict emotional discipline,

367
00:21:19,111 --> 00:21:23,540
and systematically sanitizing crime scenes
to leave virtually no physical traces.

368
00:21:23,890 --> 00:21:26,822
Investigators recognized that
a crime scene yields not only

369
00:21:26,822 --> 00:21:29,654
physical evidence such as
latent prints or blood spatter,

370
00:21:29,654 --> 00:21:31,890
but also the offender’s
Behavioral Signature.

371
00:21:32,640 --> 00:21:35,927
That was precisely what Kemper
exhibited with surgical consistency,

372
00:21:35,927 --> 00:21:38,282
from removing ballistics
from remains to burying

373
00:21:38,282 --> 00:21:40,440
a victim’s head beneath
his mother’s window.

374
00:21:40,990 --> 00:21:43,585
The insights harvested
from Kemper’s 145 IQ,

375
00:21:43,585 --> 00:21:46,356
synthesized alongside 35
other inmate dossiers,

376
00:21:46,356 --> 00:21:49,187
became the intellectual
cornerstone for cracking

377
00:21:49,187 --> 00:21:52,430
hundreds of unsolved cases
through behavioral analysis.

378
00:21:52,780 --> 00:21:56,155
They also provided enduring inspiration
for timeless cinematic works

379
00:21:56,155 --> 00:21:59,580
such as The Silence of the Lambs
and the acclaimed series Mindhunter.

380
00:22:01,580 --> 00:22:04,035
[HOST] The trial convened on October 23rd,

381
00:22:04,035 --> 00:22:06,980
1973, in Santa Cruz County Superior Court.

382
00:22:07,330 --> 00:22:12,332
Following five hours of jury deliberation,
a verdict was delivered on November 8th,

383
00:22:12,332 --> 00:22:16,490
1973, adjudging Edmund Kemper sane
and guilty across all indictments.

384
00:22:16,840 --> 00:22:19,390
Prosecutor Peter Chang
secured convictions for

385
00:22:19,390 --> 00:22:21,885
first-degree murder
across all eight victims:

386
00:22:21,885 --> 00:22:25,600
six young women, alongside his
biological mother and her companion.

387
00:22:25,950 --> 00:22:30,185
Judge Harry Brauer officially sentenced
Kemper to eight concurrent life terms —

388
00:22:30,185 --> 00:22:32,276
each ranging from seven years to life —

389
00:22:32,276 --> 00:22:36,350
rather than eight consecutive terms as
popular accounts frequently misstate.

390
00:22:36,700 --> 00:22:39,017
And in his final address to the court,

391
00:22:39,017 --> 00:22:44,140
the IQ 145 giant made an extraordinary plea
unprecedented in American legal history.

392
00:22:44,890 --> 00:22:47,844
He requested that the jury
sentence him to death by torture,

393
00:22:47,844 --> 00:22:51,290
in an effort to atone for the agony
he had inflicted upon his victims.

394
00:22:52,640 --> 00:22:53,089
However,

395
00:22:53,089 --> 00:22:57,356
because California had temporarily
suspended capital punishment at the time,

396
00:22:57,356 --> 00:23:01,229
Kemper was remanded to the California
Medical Facility in Vacaville —

397
00:23:01,229 --> 00:23:05,720
an institution serving as both maximum-security
prison and psychiatric hospital.

398
00:23:06,070 --> 00:23:09,670
Across the decades, he has
undergone dozens of parole hearings.

399
00:23:10,020 --> 00:23:13,996
At some hearings he attended, while
at others he waived his appearance;

400
00:23:13,996 --> 00:23:18,420
his petitions have been denied more than
ten times, most recently in July 2024.

401
00:23:18,770 --> 00:23:22,370
His next scheduled parole
eligibility will occur in 2031.

402
00:23:23,320 --> 00:23:27,462
During a parole hearing in 1988,
he offered a poignant reflection:

403
00:23:27,462 --> 00:23:31,040
‘Society is not ready for me,
in any way, shape, or form.

404
00:23:31,590 --> 00:23:32,990
I don’t blame them for that.’

405
00:23:35,690 --> 00:23:39,455
[HOST] Reflecting upon the tragic and
horrifying chronicle of Edmund Kemper,

406
00:23:39,455 --> 00:23:42,510
and ensuring darkness does
not claim another young life,

407
00:23:42,510 --> 00:23:46,711
let us listen to our Criminal Psychologist
and FBI Special Agent outline four

408
00:23:46,711 --> 00:23:51,130
critical Red Flags signaling the early
formation of violent personality patterns.

409
00:23:51,830 --> 00:23:55,510
[PSYCHOLOGIST] Red flag number
one: The childhood Macdonald Triad.

410
00:23:56,260 --> 00:24:01,169
These represent three classic clinical
indicators in developmental criminology:

411
00:24:01,169 --> 00:24:06,140
intentional cruelty to animals, persistent
fire-setting, and prolonged enuresis.

412
00:24:06,490 --> 00:24:11,130
In Kemper’s developmental history, the
first indicator was explicit and severe.

413
00:24:11,480 --> 00:24:14,640
At age ten, the boy
mistreated a family cat.

414
00:24:15,590 --> 00:24:19,830
Three years later, that behavior was
repeated with another household cat.

415
00:24:20,380 --> 00:24:21,038
Repetitive,

416
00:24:21,038 --> 00:24:24,684
premeditated animal abuse serves
as a primary warning sign of

417
00:24:24,684 --> 00:24:28,151
profound empathy deficits and
escalating violent conduct —

418
00:24:28,151 --> 00:24:31,140
an alarm bell tragically
ignored by his guardians.

419
00:24:32,190 --> 00:24:36,590
Red flag number two: Extreme
psychological domestic abuse.

420
00:24:37,340 --> 00:24:41,144
Confining a developing child to
isolated spaces like basements,

421
00:24:41,144 --> 00:24:43,921
subjecting them to chronic
verbal humiliation,

422
00:24:43,921 --> 00:24:46,759
or projecting gender-based
hatred onto children

423
00:24:46,759 --> 00:24:50,140
irrevocably shatters their
capacity for healthy empathy.

424
00:24:50,840 --> 00:24:55,680
[INVESTIGATOR] Red flag number three: A
violent fantasy world severed from reality.

425
00:24:56,430 --> 00:24:59,929
Exercise acute vigilance
when an adolescent displays

426
00:24:59,929 --> 00:25:03,361
habitual immersion in
violent domination fantasies,

427
00:25:03,361 --> 00:25:06,793
obsessively amasses weaponry,
or conducts isolated,

428
00:25:06,793 --> 00:25:09,350
morbid research into homicide methods.

429
00:25:09,700 --> 00:25:14,980
And red flag number four: Superior
intellect paired with shallow affect.

430
00:25:15,730 --> 00:25:20,126
A youth possessing exceptional
intellectual gifts and articulate manners,

431
00:25:20,126 --> 00:25:22,896
yet displaying complete
incapacity for genuine

432
00:25:22,896 --> 00:25:25,666
remorse or grief over
the suffering of others,

433
00:25:25,666 --> 00:25:28,978
who uses intelligence to
deceive or manipulate adults —

434
00:25:28,978 --> 00:25:32,290
that signals the protective
scaffolding of psychopathy.

435
00:25:34,290 --> 00:25:36,481
[HOST] There exists a
deeply moving human detail

436
00:25:36,481 --> 00:25:38,726
in this chronicle that we cannot overlook,

437
00:25:38,726 --> 00:25:40,970
and it belongs to a solitary prison guard.

438
00:25:41,720 --> 00:25:43,202
Throughout the 1973 trial,

439
00:25:43,202 --> 00:25:47,760
Deputy Sheriff Bruce Colomy was entrusted
with guarding and transporting Kemper.

440
00:25:48,110 --> 00:25:50,077
Amid an intensely hostile courtroom,

441
00:25:50,077 --> 00:25:52,317
Colomy was one of the few who treated the

442
00:25:52,317 --> 00:25:54,667
hulking prisoner with
professional decency,

443
00:25:54,667 --> 00:25:56,470
basic dignity, and human respect.

444
00:25:57,620 --> 00:26:01,500
That quiet kindness touched the
cold-blooded offender to his very core.

445
00:26:01,850 --> 00:26:04,232
Prior to his transfer to state prison,

446
00:26:04,232 --> 00:26:07,554
Kemper gifted Colomy his
only honorary sheriff’s pin,

447
00:26:07,554 --> 00:26:12,130
remarking with choked emotion: “To
me, he was the father I wish I’d had.”

448
00:26:13,080 --> 00:26:15,850
The dossier of Edmund Kemper
closes upon a haunting

449
00:26:15,850 --> 00:26:18,240
question regarding the
nature of human evil:

450
00:26:18,590 --> 00:26:19,999
Is a monster born wicked,

451
00:26:19,999 --> 00:26:22,423
or do catastrophic
wounds within family and

452
00:26:22,423 --> 00:26:24,790
childhood forge that beast into existence?

453
00:26:25,590 --> 00:26:28,065
Edmund Kemper entered
this world endowed with

454
00:26:28,065 --> 00:26:30,540
a genius mind and
formidable physical power —

455
00:26:30,540 --> 00:26:34,390
gifts that might have enabled him
to achieve remarkable contributions.

456
00:26:34,740 --> 00:26:38,730
Yet when those gifts were submerged
within a toxic crucible of neglect,

457
00:26:38,730 --> 00:26:40,473
relentless cruelty, and hatred,

458
00:26:40,473 --> 00:26:43,620
they mutated into an instrument
of horrific devastation.

459
00:26:44,470 --> 00:26:48,390
Locking evil behind iron bars is
the mandate of the judicial system.

460
00:26:48,740 --> 00:26:50,158
Yet identifying, sheltering,

461
00:26:50,158 --> 00:26:54,158
and healing the psychological wounds of
our children within the home... remains

462
00:26:54,158 --> 00:26:58,260
the only true path by which our society
can extinguish evil before it takes root.

463
